# Provision MacrostockStarter as device owner once the system is up. See
# device/hardkernel/common/bin/macrostock_device_owner.sh for why this cannot be done
# from inside the app.
#
# This is an exec_background rather than a service on purpose. A `service` line makes
# init compute a domain transition from u:r:init:s0 based on the executable's label, and
# /system/bin/sh is shell_exec, for which no such transition is defined. Init then
# refuses to start it with:
#
#   Could not ctl.start for 'macrostock_device_owner': File /system/bin/sh (labeled
#   "u:object_r:shell_exec:s0") has incorrect label or no domain transition from
#   u:r:init:s0 to another SELinux domain defined.
#
# and that check is enforced even when SELinux is permissive. The alternatives were to
# define a dedicated domain (see move-widevine-data-sh in
# device/hardkernel/common/sepolicy/private for that pattern) or to name the target
# domain explicitly, as here.
#
# u:r:shell:s0 / uid shell is deliberate and needs no new policy:
#   - system/sepolicy/private/init.te has domain_trans(init, shell_exec, shell), so the
#     transition is already permitted.
#   - the shell domain is already provisioned to run dumpsys and dpm and to make binder
#     calls to system_server - it is what `adb shell dpm set-device-owner` runs as.
#   - DevicePolicyManagerService.isAdb() is isShellUid(caller) || isRootUid(caller), so
#     uid shell takes the same permissive branch that the manual adb command does.
# This grants no more privilege than the adb step it replaces.
on property:sys.boot_completed=1
    exec_background u:r:shell:s0 shell shell -- /system/bin/sh /system_ext/bin/macrostock_device_owner.sh
